 effects you can add the  effective control  if you think that there's some time  variation you can add  time columns and cross terms with time  as well let's say we are trying to model  the climate system  and we want to include as a variable the  amount of co2 emissions  we might also want to include as a  variable you know solar cycles and  things like that  those would be additional external  forcing variables  that might affect the state  in the original paper we actually showed  that like control variables you can also  include parameters bifurcation  parameters or tuning parameters  into the model so you can model  discrete differential equations just as  well as continuous with cindy you just  take  discrete time data and you model the k  plus one  time step as a sparse nonlinear model of  the kth time step  and in this example we took the chaotic  logistic map  with the bifurcation parameter mu and we  measured it at 10 different  values of the mu parameter and by  including that mu  column into the cindy library so now i  can have terms that are not just  x and x squared and x cubed but mu  and mu x and mu squared x and mu x  squared  now we can learn the form of this  logistic map  even from a few noisy measurements and  then from that learned model we can  fill in the bifurcation diagram with no  noise at all parameter values because we  learned  the actual true dynamics and this all  happens in the library you have to know  kind of based on the data i'm measuring  what would i want to put in the library  do i have a parametric dependence  how might it enter my system okay very  important questions to ask  good okay um another big avenue of  research  in in cindy is trying to extend this  framework to handle larger classes of  dynamical systems  so what i showed you before allows you  to identify sparse models  in a fixed basis like polynomials sines  and cosines  bessels functions but what if you have  dynamics like this that are rational  functions so you have some polynomial on  the top  and some polynomial in the denominator  and this is work by neil mangan  when she was a postdoc with nathan and  josh  and essentially this is a very hard  class of systems to model with cindy  because these rational dynamics are not  easily expressible as a sparse  combination  of atoms or basis columns of theta  so here's kind of a toy system we were  thinking about these michaelis-menten  dynamics for chemical kinetics in  biological systems  and the idea is that if you have this  rational function  what we can do is we can multiply both  sides by the denominator  we don't know what it is yet but we  multiply it by some hypothetical  denominator  and we get a system like this and so now  we can take this dynamical system  and write it in the same way as our  original cindy problem we have a library  times a sparse vector equals zero  and now this library has to be larger it  has to include not only terms that  describe  f but it also has to have terms that  describe  f times x dots and so essentially what  that means  is that we build a library that's twice  as large it has all of the library that  theta would normally  have and then that same library times x  dot okay and now we're trying to find  the sparsest  c vector that multiplies theta to equal  zero  turns out this is a horrendous  optimization because the sparses vector  c that equals zero is zero and that's  not a very good model is the zero model  so what we ended up finding out in the  course of this paper  is that you don't just want the sparses  c you want the sparsest  c in the null space of this library  theta so this library has a null space  you want the sparse linear combination  of that null space  that gives you this c vector and it  turns out that there actually are  optimizations out there within the last  10 years to solve for this  it's pretty ill-posed because this null  space  explodes in rank if you add a little  noise to your system  but for noiseless data we can learn  very complicated chemical kinetic models  with rational terms  using this implicit cindy approach okay  very cool idea we learned a ton about  cindy and the optimization in writing  this paper with neil  and recently one of  nathan and my phd students khadir den  kamen  has essentially taken this implicit  cindy modeling procedure  and made it robust so now you're not  finding the sparses vector in a null  space  you're essentially using a much more  robust  algorithm to find these models so if you  take that same kind of michaelis-menten  dynamics here  and you're going to multiply the  denominator over  what cardi realized is that instead of  trying to write the entire implicit  equation  equals zero if you know even  a single term that is definitely in your  dynamics  you can move it over to the right hand  side and now you have a regression  that's not equal to zero  so you don't get into that null space  problem and so essentially what cardi  does  is he sweeps through all of the possible  terms that could be in the model  and he one by one moves them over and  tries that model  and if that doesn't work he moves  another one over and tries that model  and one of them  is going to work and give you an  accurate model that is also sparse  so this solves a lot of problems because  in the original  implicit cindy we had a null space  problem very ill-conditioned  by moving candidate terms over and kind  of locking them  into the model this becomes as robust as  normal cindy this has all the robustness  properties of normal cindy  which are pretty good you can handle  moderate amounts of noise  with the kind of standard cindy  algorithm and by testing all of these  terms you can do this in parallel  and you also get some interesting  consistency checks along the way  so really really cool paper i highly  encourage you to read this  uh khadiran came in has done great work  on on this robust implicit modeling  and has shown that we can model systems  that we never dreamed of modeling before  like this bz chemical reaction  so this is a four this is a coupled  system of four partial differential  equations  non-linear with rational terms this is  about as hairy as it gets in terms of  things we would want to model and with  our original cindy framework we would  have no shot at modeling this  we would need data that's way too clean  way too much of it  handling the rational terms would be a  nightmare  but with cardi's approach essentially of  you know picking  at random one of these terms and moving  it over and then doing the regression  he's able to find this very very rich  rational coupled partial differential  equation so that's super cool  a lot of applications of this and this  is hot off the presses so  you know i'm curious to see what people  use this for  in the future okay great um  so a few other things i want to talk  about in this the cindy  library problem that we're talking about  is the curse of dimensionality  so we know that one of the key strengths  of cindy is that this library  expresses many possible model  structures there are many millions of  possible models with  five or six terms out of this library  that you know could be written down and  the sparse optimization procedure with  high probability  searches through that vast model library  to find the sparsest correct model given  by the coefficients of  c that's great but  there is also another kind of cursive  dimensionality  in how many columns this theta matrix  will have  so for three variables and fifth order  polynomials it's not that bad it's  something like  81 terms but if i had 10 variables  and 10th order polynomials this theta  library would be gigantic it would have  you know hundreds of thousands i haven't  computed but  probably millions of columns and it  would be far too  ill-conditioned to take a pseudo inverse  of to do the sparse optimization on  it just wouldn't work okay and so  there's a fundamental curse of  dimensionality we want  to have this library scale well we want  to  basically have as few columns as  possible but still have the flexibility  of representing a large model space  so these are kind of dueling or  competing objectives  good okay that's uh one motivation  to reduce the size of your state x  okay so if you can find some way of  getting  less degrees of freedom to describe your  system  you probably would want to do that in  this procedure because it's going to  make your theta library  scale better now i've been saying a lot  about the condition number  of this this theta matrix and  technically i don't exactly mean  condition number  there's actually a really interesting  paper uh by joel chop  in i think 2009 it's one of the greed is  good papers  um and way in the kind of end of the  paper  in you know i don't know theorem 9 or 11  somewhere deep down i'll try to put it  in the  in the description there is a theorem  about  when you can expect to get exact  recovery of a sparse model  in a library using the lasso regression  now we're not exactly using lasso but  it's close enough  for our purposes and he introduces  a kind of more powerful or generalized  notion of condition number  called the erc condition and it  essentially is a condition number  on all sparse subspaces in this matrix  and it gives you some conditions for  when you can expect exact recovery based  on kind of a generalized condition  number of theta  turns out it's a pretty hard thing to  compute it's kind of an np hard  quantity to compute but it's good as a  thought experiment of what we care about  in this matrix  we if there is a set of columns that is  the true model  some spar set of columns if there are  other  columns in this matrix that are really  really parallel or almost linearly  dependent on those true model columns  this sparse identification is going to  be very challenging  and so that's a reason to improve the  condition number of this theta  to keep it to being as small as possible  because the more columns you add the  more linearly dependent they are  and also to add more rich transient data  to sample more initial conditions  to improve the chances of these columns  being linearly  independent it's kind of a technical  aside but very important i think about  this all the time  if you're serious about these models  you're going to have to think about it  too  your true model structure the sparse  columns you care about  can't be nearly linearly dependent on  model columns you don't care about  or else you're going to get the  misidentification  good cursive dimensionality so we've  already talked about how  one way to handle this for high  dimensional data is to take the svd the  the principal components of that data to  try to crunch it down to a smaller  feature set  that will keep your theta library  smaller uh  a deep autoencoder network is kind of  like that on steroids where you can  essentially  you know take very high dimensional data  and find as few variables  that you think are dynamic as possible  another approach that i think is really  cool is this paper  that came out i guess a couple of years  ago  by our german colleagues where  essentially what they do is they  generalize the cindy library approach  to now include tensor train formulations  for those right hand side dynamics  so instead of our generalized linear  model x dot equals  theta x times c where theta is just a  set of columns  they essentially can kind of compress  that into a tensorial representation  using these tensor trains  and allow for much much larger kind of  polynomial representations  with still being a very compact model  getting away from some of these  ill-conditioning issues and cursive  dimensionality  i'm not going to go into too many  details there's a lot to unpack here i  highly recommend you read this paper  it's fantastic  and it solves one of the most important  problems of cindy which is the curse of  dimensionality  and they show that with their kind of  tensor trained basis they identify the  correct dynamical system  whereas if you use our simple  generalized linear model sometimes you  incorrectly identify only a portion of  the dynamics so  very important paper it kind of  addresses that cursive dimensionality  and one of the last things i want to  tell you about is another way of kind of  handling that library there's a couple  cool things about the library i want to  tell you about  you can also bake in some prior  knowledge of the physics  into that theta library if i know that i  have a symmetric system  you can build your library elements to  reflect that symmetry whether it's an  odd symmetry or um you know two pi  periodic  symmetry or something like that you can  sometimes bake that into the library  so here i'm going to show you uh some  really cool work by ife guan  who exploited symmetry in this electro  convection problem  and i'll talk more about this in the  sparse fluid modeling  video but essentially he takes time  series data  reduces the dimensionality using a  principal component analysis or this svd  and then he's modeling the amplitudes of  these modes as they evolve in time  so the amplitude of the first mode is a1  the amplitude of the second mode is a2  and so on  and these are kind of the time series of  those amplitudes evolving in time  and what we realized when we looked at  this data is that it has this incredible  symmetry  you can already see that if you kind of  flip or rotate in certain ways nothing  changes about the data  and that symmetry can massively guide  how we build our theta library so  we can do this kind of constrain cindy  i'll tell you about the constraints  india in the next lecture  to get this sparse model but we  essentially constrain ourselves to have  a smaller  library and i'll show you what that  looks like here  so by taking the data and identifying  that there are some symmetries in this  case we have five key symmetries  you can determine right off the bat that  there are  many many terms in your theta library  that can't possibly  exist and exhibit those symmetries so  right off the bat  all of these black terms are ones that  we've completely crossed  out as inadmissible with these observed  symmetries  and so right away what that does is it  reduces our theta library  pretty dramatically i mean this is less  than half as big  as it would have been and it allows us  to identify  better models with less data because  there's less variables we're fitting we  have a smaller theta matrix  with a better condition number and we  can identify it with less  data because we've enforced the symmetry  okay so this is just one of the many  things you can do in the theta library  that's one of my favorite parts of cindy  is just the flexibility and the freedom  of designing the library of candidate  terms enforcing symmetries and physics  and really kind of building in your  expert intuition  for the the dynamical systems you're  trying to discover  okay good uh so we've been talking about  these key challenges  we are almost done in the next lecture  we're going to talk about the challenge  of optimization  which is kind of a fun one because there  is so much happening  in this world of optimization like this  is the the lifeblood of  machine learning well maybe data is the  lifeblood this is the engine  uh you know that's kind of making  machine learning work is all of these  optimization algorithms so that's coming  up next  all right thank you
 

